# Core workflow

## Before starting a task

1. Read [package.json](package.json) and the project importer section of [pnpm-lock.yaml](pnpm-lock.yaml).
2. Check `git status` and preserve all pre-existing user modifications.
3. Read the source files directly involved in the task, plus their imports, callers, types, and related config.
4. Do not assume a previous agent’s code is correct; verify against the actual files.

## Immediately before editing

Re-read the **complete** contents of every file you will modify. For multi-file work, inspect the full relevant implementation across layers before changing any file.

## After editing

1. Review the diff for every modified file (imports, duplicated logic, security, regressions).
2. Do not suppress TypeScript, ESLint, or test failures to make checks pass.
3. When application code changed, run `pnpm lint` and report pass, fail, or not run.
4. For UI or behavior changes, verify in the browser when browser tools are available; otherwise state what was not verified.

## Git and publishing

- Stay on the branch already checked out; do not create a new branch unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not commit, push, deploy, or rewrite history unless the user explicitly requests it. Plan or code approval is not commit approval.
- Do not change `git config` identity settings. Do not stage unrelated files.
- Never log or report credentials, tokens, or secret env values.

## Dependencies

Do not install, upgrade, downgrade, or remove packages without explaining why and getting explicit user approval.

## Intent skills

Follow the Intent block in [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md): run `pnpm exec intent list` and load a matching skill before substantial edits. If Intent is unavailable, report it; do not download a replacement.

## Scope

Make the smallest change that satisfies the request. Avoid drive-by refactors and unrelated files.

## Subagents (read-only specialists)

Project subagents live in `.cursor/agents/`. Delegate when complexity, security impact, or independent review justifies it. Do **not** invoke every specialist for trivial, localized edits, and do not delegate only to duplicate your own analysis.

| Specialist | Use when |
|------------|----------|
| `architecture-reviewer` | Significant multi-file or cross-layer design before implementation |
| `security-auditor` | Auth, authorization, validation, data exposure, uploads, secrets |
| `ui-rtl-reviewer` | Substantial storefront or admin UI (Persian RTL, a11y, states) |
| `implementation-verifier` | Before declaring significant feature work complete |

Independent specialists may run in parallel when their questions do not depend on each other. Prefer an appropriate review before calling substantial work done.

The **main agent** merges findings, resolves disagreements, implements edits, runs lint/browser/Docker checks, and reports the final result. Subagents do not waive pre-edit review, Git restrictions, dependency approval, Docker rules, or security rules.

Invoke project subagents **read-only** in this working tree only. Do not use cloud agents, Git worktrees, or parallel edits from subagents. Subagents must not create branches, commit, push, install packages, or modify files.
